26|The Package Next Door|送到隔壁的包裹|出行与办事|包裹显示送达却找不到，Lucy和爸爸查看照片后解开谜团。|核对地址、说明误送、礼貌求助
package=包裹|address=地址|doorstep=门前台阶
The photo doesn't look like our door.|Could you check the address?|Thanks for bringing it over.
lucy: Little Moments. The Package Next Door. Dad ordered a new desk lamp, and the delivery message says it has arrived. I open the front door. There's a welcome mat, a flowerpot, and no lamp. I look behind the flowerpot, even though it's much too small.
dad: Does the message include a photo?
lucy: Yes, but the photo doesn't look like our door. Our mat is green. The one in the picture says Hello in big yellow letters.
dad: That's next door. Let's check the house number on the label before we go over.
lucy: Dad calls our neighbor, Mrs. Lee. She has just found the package outside and noticed the address. A minute later, she meets us by the gate.
clerk: I was about to bring this over. Could you check the address? I think it's yours.
dad: Yes, that's our number. Thanks for bringing it over.
lucy: We carry the box inside. Dad opens it, and I help remove the paper around the lamp. Everything looks fine. We place it on my desk and turn it on.
dad: Better light for your homework.
lucy: Definitely. My potato-shaped rock now looks very important under it.
dad: Glad the lamp is serving a useful purpose.
lucy: We flatten the cardboard box for recycling. I look at the delivery photo one more time. Next time something goes missing, I'll check the details before searching behind a tiny flowerpot. Detective Lucy is learning. Slowly, but with excellent lighting.
===
27|My Turn to Choose|这次轮到我选|周末与朋友|两个朋友喜欢不同的活动，决定给彼此的选择都留时间。|表达偏好、协商顺序、遵守约定
choose=选择|turn=轮次|compromise=折中办法
What are you in the mood for?|How about we do both?|It's your turn to choose.
lucy: Little Moments. My Turn to Choose. Emma comes over on Saturday afternoon. I have a board game ready on the table. She has brought a ball. We look at each other's plans without saying anything for a moment.
emma: What are you in the mood for?
lucy: This game. I've been waiting all week to play it.
emma: I was hoping we could go outside. It's finally stopped raining.
lucy: Neither of us wants to sound difficult, so we both say whatever you want. That doesn't help at all. Then Emma looks at the clock.
emma: How about we do both? Half an hour outside, then we come in for the game.
lucy: That works. We tell Mom where we're going and walk to the little court near my house. Emma shows me a passing game that doesn't involve keeping score. Soon, I'm having too much fun to watch the time.
emma: Half an hour's up. Ready to head back?
lucy: Already? Okay. A deal is a deal. Back at home, we get water and sit down at the table. I explain the rules, and Emma asks a few questions before we start.
emma: It's your turn to choose. Which piece do you want?
lucy: The blue one. Always the blue one. Emma takes the green piece, and we begin. By the end of the afternoon, we've both tried something the other person wanted. We still disagree about which activity was better. Fortunately, nobody needs to win that argument.
===
28|The Plant on the Windowsill|窗台上的小植物|家庭日常|一家人给新植物安排照顾计划，发现不能人人都额外浇一次水。|询问是否完成、记录任务、商量照料
plant=植物|soil=土壤|care label=养护标签
Has anyone watered it today?|Let's check the care label.|I'll write it down.
lucy: Little Moments. The Plant on the Windowsill. Mom brings home a small plant in a yellow pot. We put it by the kitchen window, and Ben immediately gives it a name: Professor Leaf. The professor is quiet but seems friendly.
ben: Has anyone watered it today?
mom: I watered it when we got home. Why?
ben: I was going to give it a welcome drink.
lucy: I was thinking the same thing. Mom moves the watering can back to the shelf before Professor Leaf gets three welcomes in one afternoon.
mom: Let's check the care label. Different plants need different amounts of water. This one doesn't need a drink every time someone says hello.
lucy: We read the little label together. Mom shows us how she checks the soil and where to put a saucer under the pot.
ben: We need a schedule. Important professors have schedules.
lucy: I'll write it down. I make a small card where we can record when the plant was watered. We also agree to ask Mom before adding any more.
mom: Good. You can both help, as long as you check first.
lucy: The next morning, Ben greets the professor on his way to breakfast. He looks at the card, puts down the watering can, and gives the plant a tiny bow instead.
ben: No drink today, Professor. Just respect.
lucy: The leaves don't move. I think that means the professor approves. Or maybe he's busy preparing a lecture about why people should stop poking his soil.
===
29|A Small Apology|一句认真说的对不起|周末与朋友|Lucy不小心弄坏了朋友的书角，决定直接说明并想办法补救。|承认错误、道歉、提出补救
accident=意外|cover=封面|repair=修补
I'm really sorry.|I should have been more careful.|What can I do to help?
lucy: Little Moments. A Small Apology. Emma lends me her favorite comic book. I put it on my desk, right beside a cup of water. You can probably guess what happens next. My elbow moves, the cup tips, and the corner of the cover gets wet.
ben: Oh. That doesn't look good.
lucy: No, it doesn't. I move the cup, gently dry the book, and leave it open where it won't get knocked again. The corner still looks bent. For a minute, I wish I could pretend it was always like that.
ben: Are you going to tell Emma?
lucy: Yes. I just need to stop being nervous first. The next day, I give her the book and explain what happened. I'm really sorry. I should have been more careful.
emma: Thanks for telling me. I am disappointed, though. I really like this book.
lucy: I know. What can I do to help? I can replace it with my allowance if you'd like.
emma: Let me see how it looks when it's fully dry. Maybe we can flatten the corner. I'll tell you if I want a replacement.
lucy: Okay. I won't try to fix anything else without asking. We sit together at lunch. It feels a little awkward at first, but then Emma starts talking about the next book in the series. At home that afternoon, Ben wants an update.
ben: Are you still friends?
lucy: Yes. Friends can be disappointed and still be friends. Also, all future cups of water are getting their own table. Far away from the comic books.
===
30|The Hotel Room Key|打不开房门的房卡|出行与办事|旅行入住后发现房卡没有反应，一家人回前台求助。|描述故障、请求帮助、核对房间
key card=房卡|front desk=前台|elevator=电梯
Our key card isn't working.|Could you help us, please?|Try it one more time.
lucy: Little Moments. The Hotel Room Key. We're spending the weekend in another town. After checking in, Dad, Ben, and I take the elevator to the third floor. Ben insists on carrying the room key because it makes him feel official.
ben: Welcome to our extremely fancy vacation. Please follow me.
lucy: He taps the card against the door lock. A red light flashes. He tries again, more dramatically this time. Still red.
dad: Let's check the room number first.
lucy: The number is right. Dad tries the card, too, but the door stays closed. We take the elevator back to the lobby. Ben looks less official now.
dad: Our key card isn't working. Could you help us, please?
clerk: Of course. May I confirm the room number and the name on the reservation?
lucy: Dad gives her the details. She checks the booking and makes us a replacement card.
clerk: Here you go. Try it one more time. If there's still a problem, we'll send someone up to help.
ben: Does the card know we're on vacation now?
clerk: It should be fully informed.
lucy: Upstairs, Dad taps the new card. The light turns green, and the door opens. Ben marches in and immediately claims the bed nearest the window.
dad: Hold on. We haven't decided that yet.
lucy: Ben sits down with a sigh. Being the key-card manager, apparently, doesn't include choosing everybody's beds. Our vacation is off to a very normal family start.
===
31|The Great Pancake Flip|煎饼翻面挑战|家庭日常|周末早餐，Lucy学着耐心等煎饼做好再翻面。|按顺序操作、请求示范、分工合作
pancake=煎饼|spatula=锅铲|batter=面糊
Can you show me how?|Is it ready to flip?|Let's try a smaller one.
lucy: Little Moments. The Great Pancake Flip. Sunday breakfast smells wonderful. Mom is making pancakes, and I've decided I'm old enough to help with the pan. Ben has decided he's old enough to help with the eating. Very different jobs.
lucy: Can you show me how?
mom: Sure. Stand beside me, and keep the pan handle turned inward. I'll help you with the hot pan. You can start by pouring a small circle of batter.
lucy: I pour a circle that looks more like a sleepy cloud. We watch little bubbles appear on top. Is it ready to flip?
mom: Almost. Wait until the edges look set. Then slide the spatula underneath.
ben: I believe in you, pancake cloud.
lucy: I try to turn it, but one edge folds over. It lands looking like a blanket that someone forgot to straighten.
mom: That's okay. Let's try a smaller one. The first pancake can be a practice pancake.
ben: I volunteer to test the practice pancake.
lucy: This time, I pour less batter and wait a little longer. Mom steadies the pan while I slide the spatula underneath. One careful turn, and the pancake lands flat.
ben: Beautiful. A round breakfast miracle.
mom: Nicely done. I'll finish cooking these while you two set the table.
lucy: We bring plates, forks, and fruit to the table. Ben gets the folded pancake, just as he requested. He takes a bite and announces that it tastes exactly like a successful pancake. It simply has a more interesting shape.
===
32|The Return With a Receipt|带着小票去退换|吃饭与购物|买回来的笔记本有印刷问题，Lucy和爸爸到店里说明情况。|描述商品问题、提出换货、出示凭证
receipt=小票|exchange=换货|blank=空白的
I'd like to exchange this.|Do you have the receipt?|The pages are printed upside down.
lucy: Little Moments. The Return With a Receipt. I buy a new notebook with tiny stars on the cover. At home, I open it and discover that half the pages have the date and title boxes printed upside down. Unless I plan to write while standing on my head, something is wrong.
dad: Looks like a printing problem. We can take it back and ask about an exchange.
lucy: The next day, we return to the store. I keep the notebook and receipt together in a small bag. At the counter, I take a breath. I'd like to exchange this.
clerk: What's the problem with it?
lucy: The pages are printed upside down. I open the notebook and show her where the pattern changes.
clerk: Oh, I see. Do you have the receipt?
lucy: Yes, right here.
clerk: Thanks. We can exchange it for another one. Would you like the same design?
lucy: Definitely. I still like the stars. The clerk brings another notebook, and we check it together before I put it in my bag. All the pages look right this time.
dad: Nicely handled. You explained the problem clearly.
lucy: I was worried I would sound rude.
dad: Asking politely about a problem is okay. That's part of what the service desk is there for.
lucy: At home, I write my name on the first page. The stars are still excellent, and my words are the right way up. Ben seems disappointed. He was looking forward to the upside-down writing experiment.
===
33|The After-School Club Choice|选哪个课后社团|校园生活|Lucy和Emma想参加不同的社团，决定分别尝试再交流。|询问活动、表达兴趣、尊重不同选择
club=社团|sign up=报名|try out=尝试
What happens at the first meeting?|Do I need any experience?|We don't have to choose the same one.
lucy: Little Moments. The After-School Club Choice. The school hallway is covered with club posters. Emma stops in front of the art club sign. I stop in front of the cooking club sign. There's a picture of a very attractive muffin on it.
emma: You're choosing based on the picture, aren't you?
lucy: Partly. But I also want to learn how to make something besides toast. We walk over to the teacher at the cooking club table. What happens at the first meeting?
staff: We'll tour the kitchen, talk about the rules, and plan a simple snack together.
lucy: Do I need any experience?
staff: No. Beginners are welcome. Bring your curiosity, and we'll start with the basics.
emma: It sounds fun. But I still really want to try art club.
lucy: We don't have to choose the same one. You can show me what you make, and I can tell you what I cook.
emma: Can you also bring me what you cook?
staff: That depends on the recipe and our sharing rules. But you'll definitely have stories to exchange.
lucy: We check the meeting days and take the sign-up information home for our parents. Emma points out that both clubs meet on Wednesdays.
emma: We can still walk home together afterward.
lucy: Perfect. You'll have paint on your hands, and I'll probably have flour on my sleeves. Different clubs, same walk home. I look once more at the muffin picture. I hope my curiosity is ready, because my appetite certainly is.
